cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
title: "One Law, Two Exact Nulls: what coherence measures, and what it cannot certify"
abstract: >-
  A synthesis of two independently-run hardware programmes, containing no new
  measurements. Both are shown to measure the same function: coherence
  surviving as a product of half-angle state overlaps, one factor per party
  that has acquired distinguishing information -- one programme measuring it
  against coupling angle, the other against time. Their two internal nulls,
  each predicted and pre-registered, then settle what that function certifies:
  the same observable reads exactly zero on a separable state and on a
  maximally entangled one. Certification of quantum structure therefore
  requires agreement across incompatible measurements, a conclusion the two
  programmes reached independently from opposite directions. Includes the
  derived bounds -- Holevo capacity and the relativistic storage/throughput
  trade -- that answer the engineering questions the line began with.
type: software
version: 0.2.0-synthesis
